1. anadem_velhas.tif (increment 15c-2, 2026-10-02)
--------------------------------------------------

Source: OpenTopography's ANADEM COG,
https://opentopography.s3.sdsc.edu/raster/ANADEM/ANADEM_be/anadem_v1_compressed_COG.tif
(EPSG:4674, SIRGAS 2000), read by range requests on 2026-10-02 into the tile
cache of `rasputin fetch anadem-v1`.

Modified: cut and re-encoded. 320 x 320 nodes, the COG's rows 124232-124551
and columns 142421-142740 (upper Rio das Velhas, Minas Gerais, about
44.13-44.05 W, 19.40-19.49 S), written with the COG's key set, its spacing and
a tie point moved to the window. The elevations are the COG's, unchanged.
Written by extract.py in this directory, which is reproducible from the cache.


2. catchment.geojson (increment 15c-2, 2026-10-02)
--------------------------------------------------

Derived from anadem_velhas.tif alone, by this repository's code at commit 09c8d0e
(15c-2's green work):

  a. anadem_velhas.tif resampled by tin_engine.target_grid.resample onto its
     30 m EPSG:31983 target grid (the grid target_grid_for gives for the
     extract's node rectangle moved into EPSG:31983, default_spacing 30 m),
     written as a GeoTIFF in EPSG:31983;
  b. rasputin catchment --dem <that grid> --seed 597180 7852380
     --seed-crs EPSG:31983 --out catchment.geojson
     (no --lakes: the pour node is the grid node nearest the seed; the default
     --outline-tolerance, 60 m). It reported 11884 nodes, 10.695 km2, a
     27-vertex reduced outline, contained in the grid.

The seed was chosen by trying every fourth of the 400 lowest grid nodes at
least 25 nodes from the grid's edge as pour nodes, and keeping the largest
catchment the command did not refuse. The
command writes a one-feature FeatureCollection; the file here holds that
feature's Polygon geometry, unchanged, as a bare GeoJSON Polygon with the
same crs member (EPSG:31983), which is the form the test reads.
